// tests/sys_exit_shim.pdx — libpdx-argv.ENH-007 (Closes #14)
// SysExit::exit link-time bridge for the smoke driver.
//
// tests/smoke_driver.pdx ends every _start invocation with:
//
//   mov rdi, rax;              // rax = (pass_count << 16) | fail_count
//   call SysExit::exit;
//
// The paideia-as elaborator flattens the `SysExit::exit` path to its
// last segment (`exit`) as the linker symbol name (see
// paideia-as::parse_stmt::try_extract_symbol_name comment at line
// 1180; issue #1319). This module exports one function — `exit` —
// providing that symbol.
//
// Syscall convention
// ------------------
// paideia-os user-space and Linux both dispatch sys_exit at SC+ ID 60
// with `rdi = status` (SysV/AMD64). The bridge is trivial:
//
//   mov rax, 60;               // SC+ ID 60 = sys_exit
//   syscall;
//   ret;                       // (unreachable — sys_exit terminates)
//
// The `ret` is defensive scaffolding: sys_exit is documented as
// NORETURN and the kernel never returns control, but paideia-as's
// unsafe block encoder requires a terminating branch — `ret` is the
// unambiguous shape (see paideia-os src/user/syscall_shim.pdx:135
// for the canonical prior art).
//
// Because both hosts share the SC+ 60 dispatch (paideia-os's SC+
// numbering was chosen at R17-m1-001 to co-opt the Linux table for
// exactly this reason — a `.pdx` binary linked against this shim can
// execute either on paideia-os kernel or under a Linux userspace host
// with the same bytes), `tools/run-tests.sh` can exec the linked ELF
// directly on the developer's Linux host, no QEMU required. The exit
// status the wrapper reads is the packed (pass<<16 | fail) tally the
// smoke driver computed.
//
// Provenance
// ----------
// Pre-1.1 the smoke driver's `call SysExit::exit;` was an unresolved
// extern — every "smoke passes" claim recorded in STATUS.md,
// tests/README.md and design/architecture.md was actually a claim
// that each `.pdx` file assembled cleanly, not that the driver
// executed. This shim closes the wiring gap. See ENH-007 (#14).
//
// Link discipline
// ---------------
// A downstream tool that intends to consume libpdx-argv MUST NOT link
// this file — it exports the bare linker symbol `exit`, which would
// collide with any real tool's own exit handling. It exists solely
// for the smoke ELF built by `tools/run-tests.sh`. The build script
// keeps it out of `src/` for exactly that reason (tests/*.pdx never
// ship in the .pdxdoc or the pkg wire; they only participate in the
// per-repo smoke).
//
// paideia-as compliance
// ---------------------
//   - Module `SysExitShim` matches file basename `sys_exit_shim`
//     under `expected_module_name` (snake→PascalCase, per
//     paideia-as::file_module::expected_module_name).
//   - Leaf; no callee-save touched; no push/pop needed. rsp
//     alignment inherited from caller (smoke_driver's `push rbx`
//     put rsp at 8 mod 16 before the CALL, which the CALL itself
//     brings back to 0 mod 16 for the syscall — syscall itself
//     has no alignment requirement).
//   - `mov rax, 60` — imm32 well under 0x7FFFFFFF; encoder-safe.
//   - No `test` mnemonic (no branch here); no `and reg, imm64`;
//     no 2-op `imul r, imm`; no `cmp reg, [mem]`; no `[reg + N*8]`
//     non-unit scale. The one instruction slot (`mov rax, 60`) is
//     R49-subset by construction.
//   - `capabilities: {}` — the smoke driver already declares
//     `capabilities: {}` for `_start`, and paideia-as does not
//     perform transitive cap-chain validation across extern link
//     boundaries. Declaring `{sched}` here would be more honest
//     against the paideia-os SC+ 60 contract, but the smoke ELF's
//     runtime is Linux (which enforces no cap gate on syscall 60)
//     and the tests/ tree is per-repo, never mirror-published, so
//     the discipline layer that would consume a `{sched}` decl
//     never sees this file. Kept `{}` for consistency with the
//     rest of tests/*.pdx.

module SysExitShim = structure {

  // ============================================================
  // exit(status)
  // Signature: (u64) -> () !{sysreg} @{}
  //   rdi = status (already loaded by smoke_driver before the call)
  // Leaf; NORETURN. Emits SC+ ID 60 (sys_exit). Kernel reaps the
  // task; the wait4/waitid on the host side observes `si_status`
  // = status (full 32 bits preserved by the kernel; the low 8 bits
  // survive shell $? clamping — see tools/run-tests.sh header for
  // how the wrapper decodes the full (pass<<16 | fail) word).
  // ============================================================
  pub let exit : (u64) -> () !{sysreg} @{} =
    fn (status: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {sysreg}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "libpdx-argv.ENH-007 (Closes #14) SysExitShim::exit — SC+ ID 60 (sys_exit) trampoline for tests/smoke_driver.pdx. Two instruction slots plus a defensive `ret`. `mov rax, 60` loads the SC+ id; rdi already holds the packed (pass<<16 | fail) tally the caller (SmokeDriver::_start) computed via `shl rax, 16; or rax, rdx; mov rdi, rax` immediately before this CALL. `syscall` dispatches; the kernel never returns for sys_exit so the trailing `ret` is unreachable — kept only because paideia-as's unsafe-block encoder requires an explicit terminating branch on every path. Leaf: no callee-save reg used; no push/pop; no memory read. rsp alignment on entry is 0 mod 16 (smoke_driver's `push rbx` prologue put rsp at 8 mod 16 and the CALL pushed 8 more bytes for the return address, bringing rsp back to 0 mod 16) — syscall itself has no alignment requirement so no further bookkeeping. R49-subset compliant: no `test` (no branch), no `and reg, imm64`, no 2-op `imul r, imm`, no `cmp reg, [mem]`, no `[reg + N*8]` non-unit scale, `mov rax, 60` well under the 0x7FFFFFFF imm32 gate. Provenance: mirrors paideia-os src/user/syscall_shim.pdx:135 (sys_exit) — same SC+ id, same rdi passthrough, same {sysreg} effect. Effects `{sysreg}` correctly reflects the syscall instruction; capabilities `{}` matches the rest of tests/*.pdx (see module preamble for the discipline-layer rationale).",
      block: {
        mov rax, 60;
        syscall;
        ret;
      }
    }
}
